hello and welcome to the last agenda  before the summer break  we're here at the plenary in strasbourg  but let's take a look at what's  happening next week  at key committee meetings back in  brussels the criteria for opening a bank  account  varies and so do charges at the cost of  basic  bank services and especially  cross-border fees are particularly pesky  a new proposal on basic bank services is  the latest measure to regulate the  banking sector  by ensuring transparency of fees and  barring discrimination on the grounds of  nationality  residents and personal finances the  economic and monetary affairs committee  will consider the draft report on  tuesday  tobacco giants have lobbied and even  challenged it in court but a deal on the  tobacco products directive could be a  short whisper away  under the directive there would be a ban  on flavoured and slim cigarettes  and regulation of e-cigarettes is also  on the cards  but the biggest change is that 75 of all  packaging would have to carry a health  warning  the health committee will vote on the  draft report on wednesday  low-cost biofuels made from crops such  as rapeseed oil  are a great source of renewable energy  but studies show that they're actually  driving up food prices  and they generate more greenhouse gases  than they save  quiet in the back the eu now wants to  cap  crop-based biofuel production to 5 of  their 2020 renewable energy goals  could reduce production badly affect the  industry find out what mep's think when  the environment committee votes on the  draft report on thursday  and a quick mention of lithuania after  being passed the baton of the eu's  rotating council presidency from  ireland next week their ministers will  be busy attending committee meetings to  present  their programmes to meps  and that's all we have for this agenda  take care and enjoy the week
 

